# Inspector

- `internal/inspector.Service` owns the installation's Golem Inspector. The hub is a managed container (`docker.HubSpec`, name `golem-deploy_inspector`, label `inspector-hub=true`, found with `Client.InspectorHubs`/`IsInspectorHub` by labels and name, never by a client value). Only the daemon talks to it, over its `127.0.0.1` port with the fixed origin `HubOrigin` and host `hubHost`. Never embed the hub package, never change the engine, never put the hub on a network shared with games.
- Hub secrets (`hubSecrets`: password and its cost-12 bcrypt hash, hub agent token, per-server token key, generation) are generated on first enable or `rotate_secrets`, sealed with `secrets.InspectorSecretsPurpose` in `settings.inspector_secrets` (durable write) and only reach the hub's env file. Never return, log or put them in argv or labels; config hashes use the generation. Per-server tokens are `gdi_<id>_<hex HMAC-SHA256>` (`agentToken`, constant-time `verifyAgentToken`).
- `inspector.apply` (restricted, subject `inspector/hub`, `Update` validates, starts it, then stores `settings.inspector_enabled`): secrets, `EnsureNetwork`, image (`ResolveImage`; `PullImage` with the dedicated build client configuration only when missing or `pull_image`; the automatic repair never pulls), keep a running hub whose `config-hash` label and image match `hubHash` or replace it, `waitHubReady`, daemon login, open the listener. Off: close the listener, end sessions and agents, remove the hub. Failures set a user-safe `detail`; Docker stderr goes to the daemon log only; hub output goes to the job log through `logsafe` with `hub | ` and secrets redacted. `Run` repairs at start and at most every `RepairInterval` after an attempt, sweeps sessions every `SweepInterval`, and keeps the hub port current (`refreshHub`); `Close` ends everything at shutdown, the hub keeps running.
- The listener is bound only while the setting is on. With panel TLS it serves the panel certificate to browsers and plaintext to agents on one port (`sniffer`, first byte 0x16, bounded wait); without TLS one plaintext server does both. Browser requests use the shared `netpolicy.Policy` (Host allowlist plus the `--inspector-public-url` host, `ClientIP`). The agent endpoint skips the Host policy and checks the peer (managed network subnets or loopback) instead.
- Browser side: one-time tickets (32 random bytes, hashed, 60 s, bounded per account and in total) from `CreateSession`; `GET /_golem-deploy/enter` (GET only, failures limited per client address) logs in at the hub for a new daemon session and sets `golem_deploy_inspector`. Every request rechecks the account (exists, approved, same token version); 8 h lifetime; sign-out forwards to the hub. The proxy strips cookies, credentials and forwarding headers, injects only the session's hub cookie, rewrites Origin, drops `Set-Cookie`, never forwards `/auth/login`, `/ws/agent`, `/metrics`, and requires the browser's own Origin for WebSockets and non-GET/HEAD. A lost hub session (401, or `/auth/session` false) is renewed once per request through `relogin`; `loginGuard` stops logins after a hub 401 (until the next apply) or 429 (until Retry-After). Ended sessions cancel their tracked requests, which closes open WebSocket streams.
- Agent side: relay `/ws/agent` with the hub's token instead of the server's, at most `MaxAgentsPerServer` per server and `MaxAgents` in total; `agents.closeAll` on off and rotation.
- `Agent`/`AgentToken` implement `servers.Inspector`; the servers rule covers injection and the config hash. `GET /api/v1/inspector` (`Status`) reads the hub container and, through a daemon-held hub session, `/api/targets` (cached `targetsTTL`, bounded body, only bounded fields relayed, matched by server name among servers with the switch on).
- Verify with `go test ./internal/inspector/... ./internal/netpolicy/... ./internal/httpapi/...`; with Docker, `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1 go test -p 1 ./internal/inspector/` builds a stand-in hub image (`inspectortest/standinhub`), applies it for real, checks hardening and labels, enters, proxies and relays an agent from a container.
